    <p>The DG row starts at 2,500 lb per loose cubic yard, or 1.25 US short tons. It is a planning preset, not an ASTM value. <a href="https://www.gravelshop.com/shop/calculate-cubic-yard-feet-ton.asp?groupid=30&amp;productid=819" rel="noopener noreferrer">Gravelshop lists one ½-inch-minus DG at 2,410 lb/yd³</a>, while <a href="https://www.outdoorwarehousesupply.com/product/decomposed-granite/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Outdoor Warehouse Supply lists its ¼-inch-minus DG at 2,700–3,000 lb/yd³</a>. The example range rounds those supplier figures to about 2,400–3,000 lb/yd³ (89–111 lb/ft³). Use the measured or quoted density for your specific product.</p>

    <h2>Why Densities Vary: The Three Field Factors</h2>
    <p>
      Experienced civil engineers and contractors understand that bulk density is not a single static number. True field densities fluctuate based on three key physical variables:
    </p>

    <h3>1. Mineral Specific Gravity</h3>
    <p>
      The parent geology of the quarry dictates base mineral density. Igneous basalt and granite naturally possess higher solid mineral specific gravities (2.65 to 2.80) than porous sedimentary limestone (2.50 to 2.65) or volcanic scoria (1.40 to 1.70).
    </p>

    <h3>2. Moisture Content (Rainfall Absorption)</h3>
    <p>
      Aggregates stored in outdoor quarry stockpiles absorb surface moisture during rain events. Wet crushed stone can weigh <strong>5 to 8 percent more per cubic yard</strong> than dry stone. When ordering by certified scale weight after heavy rainfall, slight volumetric adjustments occur.
    </p>

    <h3>3. Loose vs Compacted State</h3>
    <p>
      Dense-graded aggregates such as crusher run or decomposed granite can occupy less volume after compaction. That changes their bulk density, but the change depends on gradation, moisture, equipment, and site conditions. Use a supplier's loose-to-finished allowance when estimating material for a specified finished depth.
    </p>
